 Given the equation $\mathbf{a} \times (\mathbf{a} \times \mathbf{b}) = -3 \mathbf{b}$ and the vector identity $\mathbf{a} \times (\mathbf{a} \times \mathbf{b}) = \mathbf{a}(\mathbf{a} \cdot \mathbf{b}) - \mathbf{b}(\mathbf{a} \cdot \mathbf{a})$.
Consider the dot product $\mathbf{a} \cdot \mathbf{a}$:
\begin{align*}
\mathbf{a} \cdot \mathbf{a} &= (\mathbf{a} \cdot \mathbf{b})\mathbf{a} + 3\mathbf{a} \\
&= \mathbf{a}(\mathbf{a} \cdot \mathbf{b}) + 3\mathbf{a}
\end{align*}
Next, consider the cross product $\mathbf{a} \times \mathbf{b}$. Let $\mathbf{n}$ be a vector perpendicular to both $\mathbf{a}$ and $\mathbf{b}$. Then, $\mathbf{a} \times \mathbf{b} = \pm\mathbf{n}$, and thus $|\mathbf{a} \times \mathbf{b}| = |\mathbf{n}|$. Since $\mathbf{n}$ is perpendicular to $\mathbf{a}$, $\mathbf{a} \times \mathbf{b} \times \mathbf{a} = -\mathbf{b}$.
Applying the vector identity:
\begin{align*}
\mathbf{a} \times (\mathbf{a} \times \mathbf{b}) &= \mathbf{a}(\mathbf{a} \cdot \mathbf{b}) - \mathbf{b}(\mathbf{a} \cdot \mathbf{a}) \\
&= \mathbf{a}(\mathbf{a} \cdot \mathbf{b}) - (\mathbf{a} \cdot \mathbf{b})\mathbf{a} + 3\mathbf{a} \\
&= -\mathbf{b}
\end{align*}
Therefore, $\mathbf{a}$ is a non-zero vector perpendicular to $\mathbf{b}$ with $\|\mathbf{a}\| = 3\|\mathbf{b}\|$.
